C = [
("h1", "12. Colossians 2:8–23: Let No Man Judge You"),
("p", "Claim: Colossians 2:8–23 gives a command to the opposite party from Romans 14. In Romans the believer is told not to judge a brother over meats and days. In Colossians the believers are told not to let anyone judge them in meat, drink and days. The two passages leave the same matters open, and between them they close the door in both directions: no one is to be judged, and no one is to impose."),
("sub", "12.1 The setting"),
("p", "The letter is written “to the saints and faithful brethren in Christ which are at Colosse” (Colossians 1:2), and the passage begins with a warning about a teaching. “Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men, after the rudiments of the world, and not after Christ” (Colossians 2:8). The ground of the warning follows: “For in him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead bodily. And ye are complete in him, which is the head of all principality and power” (Colossians 2:9, 10). The believers are told that they lack nothing. The false teaching proposes to supply a lack."),
("p", "Verses 11 to 15 are the reason the teaching has no place: the circumcision made without hands, the burial and rising “with him,” the forgiveness of trespasses, the blotting out of “the handwriting of ordinances” (Colossians 2:11–14), and the triumph over principalities and powers (Colossians 2:15). The volume cites these verses and does not read them. The handwriting of ordinances is read in the volume on the Cross in Paul’s letters, beside the Law’s jurisdiction in Study 59. The burial with him in baptism is read in Study 42. What the volume needs from them is only that Paul gives them as the ground of what follows."),
("sub", "12.2 The command"),
("p", "“Let no man therefore judge you in meat, or in drink, or in respect of an holyday, or of the new moon, or of the sabbath days: which are a shadow of things to come; but the body is of Christ” (Colossians 2:16, 17). The command has the word “therefore,” so it rests on verses 8 to 15. Five matters are named: meat, drink, an holyday, the new moon, the sabbath days. The command is that no man is to judge the believers in them. The reason is that they “are a shadow of things to come; but the body is of Christ.” Constable puts it, “The dietary and festival observances were like shadows of Christ.”"),
("p", "The five matters are the matters of Israel’s calendar and table. “To offer all burnt sacrifices unto the LORD in the sabbaths, in the new moons, and on the set feasts” (1 Chronicles 23:31). “I will also cause all her mirth to cease, her feast days, her new moons, and her sabbaths, and all her solemn feasts” (Hosea 2:11). The sabbaths are there with the new moons and the feasts. Paul’s list in Colossians follows the same course. The weekly sabbath was given to Israel as “a sign between me and the children of Israel for ever” (Exodus 31:17), “a perpetual covenant” (Exodus 31:16). The volume holds that the Body is in no Israelite covenant, and that no day is therefore commanded to it. The Sabbath passages are read in Study 78, which reports them as written. This volume argues a conclusion from Colossians 2:16 and Romans 14:5, 6 together, and chapter 17 gives the strongest objection."),
("sub", "12.3 Romans 14 and Colossians 2 together"),
("p", "The two passages are written to different churches, and their direction differs. In Romans 14 two believers differ and each is told how not to treat the other: the eater is not to despise, the abstainer is not to judge. In Colossians 2 the pressure comes from outside the company of believers who agree: “let no man … judge you.” The volume reads the second as the answer to a person who would impose his scruple as a rule. If the weak man of Romans 14 began to tell the strong that they must keep his days or his meats, Colossians 2 would apply to him. If the strong man of Romans 14 began to tell the weak that they must eat, Romans 14 would apply to him. The two passages do not conflict. They are the two sides of one boundary."),
("sub", "12.4 The rest of the passage"),
("p", "“Let no man beguile you of your reward in a voluntary humility and worshipping of angels, intruding into those things which he hath not seen, vainly puffed up by his fleshly mind, and not holding the Head, from which all the body by joints and bands having nourishment ministered, and knit together, increaseth with the increase of God” (Colossians 2:18, 19). The second command is against the one who would beguile the believers of their reward by false humility and the worship of angels. The volume reports the verse as written. It names the fault as “not holding the Head,” which is the ground of the whole chapter."),
("p", "“Wherefore if ye be dead with Christ from the rudiments of the world, why, as though living in the world, are ye subject to ordinances, (Touch not; taste not; handle not; which all are to perish with the using;) after the commandments and doctrines of men?” (Colossians 2:20–22). The ordinances here are prohibitions of food and contact, and Paul calls them “the commandments and doctrines of men.” Constable notes that the false teachers “wanted to limit it by prohibiting certain perfectly legitimate activities.” The last verse of the chapter passes a verdict on them: “Which things have indeed a shew of wisdom in will worship, and humility, and neglecting of the body; not in any honour to the satisfying of the flesh” (Colossians 2:23). They have an appearance of wisdom and no value against the flesh."),
("sub", "12.5 Why the line matters for liberty"),
("p", "Colossians 2 shows what Paul does not mean by the limit on liberty. The limit set for the sake of a brother is not a set of rules. Paul does not tell the strong that they are now under “Touch not; taste not; handle not.” He tells them to give up a use of their liberty when a brother would stumble, and he tells the Colossians that no man is to impose such rules. The distinction is between a limit a believer takes on himself for a brother’s sake, and a rule laid on believers by men. Paul commends the first and condemns the second."),
("ctbl", "Colossians 2:8–23 by the five questions.", [
    ("Question", "Answer from the passage", "Verse"),
    ("Does Paul command?", "Yes: beware; let no man judge you in meat, drink or days; let no man beguile you", "2:8, 16, 18"),
    ("What limit?", "No man may judge or impose rules on the believers in these matters", "2:16, 20–22"),
    ("To whom is it owed?", "To Christ the Head, from whom the body has nourishment", "2:19"),
    ("What ground?", "Ye are complete in him; these are a shadow, but the body is of Christ; they are commandments of men", "2:10, 17, 22"),
    ("What is unsaid?", "What the false teachers taught in full; what the “reward” is; which days are meant beyond the list", "Chapter 16"),
  ], [0.26, 0.52, 0.22]),
("sub", "12.6 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "Paul commands the Colossians not to let anyone judge them in meat, drink, holyday, new moon or sabbath days.",
    "The reason given is that they are complete in Christ, and that these things are a shadow of things to come.",
    "With Romans 14:5, 6 the passage leaves days to conscience for the Body; the Sabbath as a sign to Israel is left to Israel’s covenant.",
    "The limit set for a brother’s sake and the rule laid by men are different things, and Paul commends the one and condemns the other.",
  ]),
]
